#
# The USB keyboard and mouse at boot: cadr-usb-input reading the board's own
# USB host port and sending what it finds to cadr-terminal, which writes the
# I/O board's registers.
#
# S88, after S85cadr-terminal, because the terminal is what listens on the
# link.  Started before it, this program says the link is not there and tries
# again every second, so the order is tidiness rather than a dependency --- but
# a console log that reads in the order the machine needs things is worth the
# number.
#
# **NO OPTION HAS TO BE PASSED AND A BOARD USUALLY PASSES NONE.**  The program
# looks in /dev/input for a keyboard and a mouse, opens what is there, and
# keeps looking, so one init script is right for a board with a keyboard
# plugged in, one with a keyboard plugged in later, and one with nothing
# plugged in at all.  What a card does want to say it says in `fpgarc`, below.
#
# **THE DEVICES ARE NOT TAKEN EXCLUSIVELY.**  `--grab` would keep the keys from
# anything else on the board, and nothing else on this board reads them: there
# is no virtual console, the login is on the serial line, and the keyboard is
# there to type at the CADR.  Off by default all the same, because a grab that
# succeeds on a device somebody is debugging with `evtest` is a keyboard that
# has silently stopped answering them.
#
# The log goes to the console, which is where the board is watched from
# (docs/boot.md).  start-stop-daemon's -b would send it to /dev/null, so the
# program is told where to write instead.

PIDFILE=/var/run/cadr-usb-input.pid
PACKS=/mnt/packs

# **THE CARD'S ONE FILE OF FLAGS, FILTERED TO THIS PROGRAM'S.**  `fpgarc`
# configures the CADR in the fabric the way `muirrc` beside it configures the
# CADR inside muir: one flag a line, the flag then a space then the rest of
# the line as its argument, `#` a comment, carriage returns stripped.  Several
# programs serve this machine and each refuses a flag it does not know, so the
# file is not passed to any of them whole --- cadr-common's reader hands back
# the lines whose flag is in the list below and nothing else.  Its header is
# the contract and docs/fpgarc.md has every program's list.
#
# S80cadr-disk-packs mounts that partition before this runs.  A boot where the
# mount failed still starts this and takes the defaults, which are what the
# program does with no flags at all.
RC="$PACKS/fpgarc"
FPGARC_SH=/usr/share/cadr/fpgarc.sh
. "$FPGARC_SH"

# **THE FLAGS THIS PROGRAM OWNS**, and it is the `--usb-` spellings alone.
# The program takes every flag twice --- `--device` and `--usb-device`, and so
# on --- and src/cadr-usb-input.c says the second spelling is there for this
# file.  That is the rule a file serving several programs needs: `--device`,
# `--grab` and `--no-mouse` are words another program could want, and a flag
# in `fpgarc` names one program or it names none.  `--link` and `--usb-link`
# are the same seam cadr-terminal listens on, and the `--usb-` one is this
# end of it.  `--log` and `--once` are this script's or a person's at a
# prompt.
FLAGS="--usb-link
       --usb-device
       --usb-input-dir
       --usb-scan-ms
       --usb-grab
       --usb-no-keyboard
       --usb-no-mouse"

start() {
	set --
	[ -f "$RC" ] && eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" $FLAGS)"

	printf "Starting cadr-usb-input: "
	start-stop-daemon -S -q -b -m -p "$PIDFILE" \
		--exec /usr/bin/cadr-usb-input -- "$@" --log /dev/console
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

stop() {
	printf "Stopping cadr-usb-input: "
	start-stop-daemon -K -q -p "$PIDFILE"
	[ $? = 0 ] && echo "OK" || echo "FAIL"
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
